  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Night-Assessor Cevmucsiatum-larv~
    
    A Nightshelf merchant and contract assessor operating from the lower markets of The Drowned Ledge, the partially flooded mining outpost whose official history is maintained by parties not known for honesty. Cevmucsiatum-larv~ co-founded the Collective☽ Of Led∴merch alongside ☽'tenebris during the Clever Ice Age, building the guild's shadow-commerce network at a time when Nightshelf trade required channels beyond Aurora Stack oversight. His assessments — readings of contracts with an acuity compared, not without reason, to Aurora crystal-divination — became the commercial backbone of the Collective's operations.
    
    Something altered during the Clever Ice Age. The sources are characteristically vague. The summary record states he "witnessed something in the ice," and the proverb tradition of The Drowned Ledge encodes the event obliquely: *the assessor with the shaking flipper, whose ledger read him back.* His left flipper now tremors when holding a stylus for extended work. He wears a cracked fire-core amulet — its light long dead — that he has never replaced. He has begun refusing contracts without explanation — a practice that, in Nightshelf commercial culture, signals either profound conviction or profound instability, and his partners cannot determine which.
    
    He possesses the ability to read thermal memory-impressions from deep ice by pressing against the substrate — an ice-sight that extracts historical thermal data from frozen stone, including process-traces from makers who preceded the Collective by centuries. Sustained readings carry significant physical cost; the tremor climbs from his left flipper into his shoulder.
    
    He conducts himself with studied visibility: flippers always in sight, never crossed, never concealed. His speech defaults to old proverb-code — *'The ice remembers what the ledger forgets'* — which functions less as wisdom than as a boundary marker. His leather satchel bears no guild-marks. Other merchants avoid his tables after dark.
    
    The cautionary sayings collected in *Sayings of the Ice* reference him without naming him directly, which is itself a Nightshelf convention worth noting: what the proverbs decline to name, they consider still active.
    
    His assessments still carry weight sufficient to give guild-masters pause. Whether this reflects his skill or what now stands behind it is a question the record does not answer.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771591262952_0", anchor: "The sources are characteristically vague", text: "\"Characteristically vague\" is my phrasing, doing my evasion for me. The Clever Ice Age timeline places Cevmucsiatum-larv~ at The Drowned Ledge during or immediately before the breach — when the deepest tunnel opened into something that shouldn't have held water. The Foundation Depths preserve impressions at comparable strata that predate penguin civilization entirely. A tremoring flipper, a cracked fire-core worn against the body, assessments carrying weight beyond professional skill, refusal patterns consistent with someone who no longer trusts his own continuity of judgment. I described this constellation in another entry and stopped one sentence short of the word. I have done so again.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771591262952_1", anchor: "what the proverbs decline to name, they consider still active", text: "Or still dangerous. ☽'Cilocum's correspondence manuals distinguish between unnamed-because-potent and unnamed-because-unsettled. The latter carries obligation — specifically, the obligation not to resolve the naming until the subject resolves themselves. The difference matters here.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
